From 3ea12ebd936ae45aaec4655286ebbcc8ded41839 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: patricksmithlaravel <patricksmithlaravel@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 21:57:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ios: report a lone Return or Tab insertion as a named key

UIKit hands typed text to a UIKeyInput view one insertText: call at a
time. The software keyboard's Return key and a hardware keyboard's Return
and Tab keys insert "\n" and "\t" alone, but every inserted character
became a Key::Character, so on iOS Return arrived as Character("\n") and
Tab as Character("\t"), where the other backends report NamedKey::Enter
and NamedKey::Tab. Code that acts on NamedKey::Enter (submitting a text
field, breaking a line in an editor) never saw Return on iOS.

It cannot be recovered from the events either: nothing marks where one
insertion ends, and when UIKit delivers several insertions in one turn of
the run loop (a Return typed right after a letter while the app is busy),
"x" then "\n" looks the same as an inserted "x\n".

insertText: now reports an insertion of only "\n", "\r" or "\r\n" as one
press and release of NamedKey::Enter (physical key KeyCode::Enter), and
one of only "\t" as NamedKey::Tab (KeyCode::Tab), as deleteBackward
reports NamedKey::Backspace with KeyCode::Backspace. The press's text is
the inserted text. Any other insertion (dictation, a keyboard suggestion,
a third-party keyboard) is text as before: a Key::Character per
character, line breaks and tabs included, so inserted multi-line text
does not press Enter halfway.

To be proposed upstream; there is no pull request yet.

Verified: cargo +nightly fmt --check on the file; cargo clippy
-D warnings for aarch64-apple-ios-sim. The new unit tests, built with
cargo test --target aarch64-apple-ios-sim --lib --no-run and run in an
iOS 27.0 simulator with xcrun simctl spawn: 10 passed. With the old
per-character behaviour, a_lone_line_break_is_return, a_lone_tab_is_tab
and a_return_after_a_letter_stays_a_return fail. In an iced app on the
simulator that calls insertText: with "x", "\n", "one\ntwo" and "\t" in
one turn of the run loop, the keys were Character("x"), Named(Enter),
the characters of "one\ntwo", Named(Tab); before, "\n" and "\t" came as
characters.
